                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_smart:         `<strong>That's the diagnosis the post-mortems would have made.</strong> All the analytical horsepower in the world, and the pod still stalls because no one aligns the client, surfaces disagreement, or owns a decision. The dominant blocker is <strong>smart</strong> — and naming it is the whole game.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<span class="sharp">“It needs more analytical firepower”</span> is exactly the reflex that loses this account. The model is at 0.84 against a 0.75 target — it was never the problem. Push the AUC and the steering committee rejects the readout a third time, for the same reason. What is this pod <em>actually</em> missing?`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Just tired, add hours” misreads it.</strong> The pod isn't lazy; it's mis-deployed. More undifferentiated effort poured onto a smart-skill gap moves nothing and burns the budget you don't have. Where is the effort going — the binding constraint or the comfortable one?`,
                    coach_r1_translation:   `<strong>That's the gap the board will expose.</strong> A 0.84-AUC model rejected twice as “practically useless” because no one converted it into decisions Bumi can take. Closing the translation gap is a <strong>smart</strong>-skill task — framing, narrative, stakeholder empathy — not a sharper model.`,
                    coach_r1_auc:           `<span class="sharp">“0.84 isn't high enough”</span> is the trap dressed as diligence. The target was 0.75; you have beaten it comfortably. The board hasn't rejected the readout because the model is weak — they rejected it because nobody told them what to <em>do</em> with it.`,
                    coach_r2_owner:         `<strong>A named single owner is the unlock.</strong> “The whole pod manages Bumi” is why the sponsor's requirements kept shifting unchallenged — diffuse ownership reads to the client as no ownership. One accountable owner of the relationship, and scope-drift finally meets a wall.`,
                    coach_r2_datalead:      `<strong>The sharp lead fronting the client doubles down on the wrong skill family.</strong> Your strongest <span class="sharp">analyst</span> is not necessarily your strongest <strong>stakeholder manager</strong> — and the gap here is stakeholder management. Re-role to the gap, not the title.`,
                    coach_r2_ownerless:     `<strong>“The whole pod will manage Bumi together” is why nobody does.</strong> Diffuse ownership reads to the client as no ownership — it's exactly how the sponsor's scope drifted unchallenged. Who, <em>by name</em>, owns Bumi from tomorrow?`,
                    coach_r2_more:          `<span class="sharp">More tuning on an already-strong model</span> is RM spent making 0.84 marginally stronger while the real gap stays wide open. Freeze the model; the perfectionism is avoidance of the harder, smarter conversation. Redirect that horsepower to the sizing the board will demand.`,
                    coach_r2_actionlearning: `<strong>That's action learning.</strong> The pod gets better at smart skills by doing the real alignment work — owning the client, surfacing the conflict, building the decision-led readout — not by attending a seminar. The live Bumi prep <em>is</em> the development vehicle, and it respects the deadline.`,
                    coach_r2_seminar:       `<strong>A seminar series teaches smart skills in the abstract while the real one goes unpractised.</strong> Padurean's point is that people develop most by working real problems with real stakes. There's a live, high-stakes problem in front of you — use <em>it</em>.`,
                    coach_r2_offsite:       `<strong>A three-day offsite is well-intentioned and eats the deadline.</strong> The readout is in 21 days and you've just spent three of them in a workshop instead of doing the alignment work live. Action learning means learning <em>through</em> the real task — could the readout prep itself be the intervention?`,
                    coach_r2_nointervention: `<strong>“No time, just produce” leaves the smart gap exactly where it was.</strong> Producing harder against an unaddressed human-collaboration gap is how the readout got rejected twice. The intervention doesn't have to cost the deadline — but doing nothing guarantees the third rejection.`,
                    coach_r3_sharpheavy:    `<span class="sharp">You poured the effort into the analytical work — the skill the pod already has in abundance.</span> Adding more of an abundant skill yields little or negative return. The binding constraint is smart; effort spent on sharp here doesn't just waste time, it <em>destroys</em> value while the real gap widens.`,
                    coach_r3_conflictcoach: `<strong>Coaching the consultants to surface it productively is the smart-skill move.</strong> You didn't avoid the conflict or do it for them — you built their capability to push back on the sponsor without rupturing the relationship. That's how the scope-drift stops <em>and</em> the gap closes for good.`,
                    coach_r3_leadtakes:     `<strong>You confronting the sponsor solves today's scope but not the gap.</strong> Going over the consultants' heads gets the requirement frozen, yet leaves them exactly as conflict-avoidant tomorrow. The smart skill is theirs to build — back them to do it, don't do it for them.`,
                    coach_r3_conflicthope:  `<strong>“Leave them to work it out” is conflict avoidance — and it isn't neutral.</strong> They won't work it out; that's the smart-skill gap. The sponsor changed scope again yesterday and nobody pushed back. Avoiding conflict is exactly how the scope drifted in the first place.`,
                    coach_r3_weekends:      `<strong>Weekend work and longer hours — and burn just climbed while value barely moved.</strong> You're going <em>backwards</em> on margin. The pod isn't lazy; it's mis-deployed. Hours thrown at the comfortable constraint don't reach the binding one — redeploy the skill, don't pile on the effort.`,
                    coach_r3_dosehigh:      `<strong>Too heavy a dose — you've spent the deadline on “learning.”</strong> Action learning happens <em>through</em> the task, not instead of it. A big realignment block this late leaves too little runway to deliver. Dial it down and let the learning happen in the real prep.`,
                    coach_r3_doselow:       `<strong>Almost no learning or realignment — you're just producing harder.</strong> That leaves the smart gap exactly where it was and risks a third rejected readout. A moderate dose, woven through the real work, develops the capability without costing the deadline.`,
                    coach_r3_skillfit:      `<strong>That's skill-fit over effort.</strong> Tilt toward the smart work that's actually missing, coach the consultants through the conflict, redeploy rather than pile on hours, and let a moderate learning dose ride the real task. Progress moves because the right skill is finally on the binding constraint.`,
                    coach_r4_reframe:       `<strong>Holding the diagnosis under pressure is the test — and you passed it.</strong> The model is done at 0.84; one more variant is the sponsor's anxiety, not the gap. Reframing buys the last week for the translation work that actually lands the readout.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Quietly not doing it and saying nothing is avoidance wearing a disguise.</strong> The honest move is to reframe <em>with</em> the sponsor — the model is done, the gap is decisions. Ducking the conversation just lets the same unsurfaced tension fester into the readout.`,
                    coach_r4_comply:        `<span class="sharp">Pulling the team back onto modelling under pressure is the regression the whole simulation warns against.</span> AUC creeps from 0.84 to 0.86, burn climbs, and the translation work — the actual gap — gets no closer. The board rejects the readout a third time, for the same reason.`,
                    coach_r4_decisions:     `<strong>Decisions first — that's the spine the board buys.</strong> What Bumi should do on Monday and what the model says it's worth, with the analysis backing each call. You've finally closed the translation gap that got the first two readouts rejected.`,
                    coach_r4_hybrid:        `<strong>Recommendations bolted on at the end still bury the lede.</strong> A board that rejected AUC twice will read “methodology first, decisions if there's time” as the same data-dump in a nicer suit. Lead with the decision; let the model serve it.`,
                    coach_r4_datadump:      `<strong>Fourteen slides of model performance and “Recommendations: TBD” is the third version of the same mistake.</strong> The board doesn't buy AUC; they buy decisions. Rewrite the spine: what should Bumi <em>do</em> on Monday, and what does the model say it's worth?`,
                    coach_r4_translate:     `<strong>That's the translation gap closed.</strong> “Here are the three churn-reduction moves, what each is worth, and the decision we need today” — framing, narrative and stakeholder empathy turning a strong model into action. That's a <strong>smart</strong>-skill, and it's what lands a flagship.`,
                    coach_r4_apologise:     `<strong>Apologising and asking for more time concedes the engagement.</strong> The steering committee doesn't want contrition; they want decisions they can act on. Lead with the translated recommendations and the apology becomes unnecessary.`,
